I have a patch to announce, which I'll call K Reform.

Ideally, each color/symbol combination would uniquely identify a monster,
so that ASCII players recieve the same information as Tile players. That's
the case for many monsters, but there is still much overlap.

The worst case is "@", where you have soldiers, elves, human-form
werebeasts, quest characters, and various miscellaneous humans all piled
on one symbol, using overlapping color codes.

I'm not going to try to untie the whole knot -- indeed that probably
isn't possible without giving really unintuitive symbol assignments, or
extending the character set.  But I see one chance to loosen it, by
spilling some monsters over into the "K" category, which is underused and
contains monsters most players never see.

I've decided that watchmen, watch captains, and (vault) guards qualify as
police of a sort, so I moved them into K as grey, green, and yellow,
respectively.  I then decided Croesus could follow his guards, so he is
moves from the very crowded purple-@ to a white-K.  Finally, I moved the
(lowest level) Keystone Kop to black so that each level has it's own
color.

Summary:

Monster         Was       Becomes    Resolving ambiguity with
-------------   -------   -------    ------------------------
Keystone Kop    Blue K    Black K    Kop Sergeant
Watchman        Grey @    Grey K     Grey-elf, Soldier
Watch Captain   Green @   Green K    Woodland-elf, Lieutenant,
                                     Neferet the Green
Guard           Blue @    Yellow K   Captain
Croesus         Magenta @ White K    Elvenking, Wizard of Yendor,
                                     13 quest leaders/nemesi.

My patch fixes things up so that this does not change the actual
properties of the monsters involved, with one exception: now a
blessed-genocide of "K" can selectively destroy the minetown watch.  (The
other moved monsters are genocideproof.)

I've used it myself for a while without noticing a problem, but I never
summon the kops.

It's on ftp.ocis.net as
"/pub/users/ldeutsch/beta/nethack-3.4.3-kreform.diff".

